1) What is UAT? Who will do the testing and what is the purpose of testing?

User Acceptance Testing (UAT) is the final phase of software testing in which actual users, clients,
or customers test the application to verify that it meets their business requirements and
expectations.

Performs UAT:
• Customers
• Clients
• Business users
• End users

Purpose of UAT:
• To ensure the software satisfies business needs.
• To verify that all requirements are implemented correctly.
• To confirm that the application is ready for deployment.
• To gain approval from the client before release.

Example: 
  In an online shopping application, users verify whether they can search products, add
items to the cart, make payments, and receive order confirmations successfully.

2) What is the difference between Functional and Non functional testing ? 

Functional Testing checks what the system does and validates application features and functions
based on business requirements.
Examples: Login, Registration, Search, Payment Processing.

Non-Functional Testing checks how the system performs and validates quality attributes such as
performance, security, reliability, and usability.
Examples: Load Testing, Performance Testing, Security Testing, Usability Testing

3) Load testing will come under functional or non functional testing? And why it is coming under that category?

Load Testing is a type of Non-Functional Testing.
It evaluates how the application behaves when a large number of users access it simultaneously. It
measures response time, system stability, server performance, and the application's ability to
handle user load.
Example: Testing an e-commerce website with 10,000 users logging in at the same time.

4) What is Exploratory Testing and when we will do this testing ?

Exploratory Testing is a testing approach where the tester explores the application, learns its
behavior, and performs testing simultaneously without predefined test cases.

 performed:
• When requirements are incomplete or unclear.
• When documentation is limited.
• When quick testing is required.
• To discover unexpected defects.
• During the early stages of testing.

Advantages:
• Finds hidden defects.
• Improves understanding of the application.
• Useful for newly developed features.

5) What is Black box Testing and how it differs from White box testing ?

Black Box Testing is a testing method where the tester verifies the functionality of the application
without knowing the internal code or program logic.
Features:
• Focuses on inputs and outputs.
• No programming knowledge required.
• Usually performed by testers.

White Box Testing is a testing method where the tester examines the internal code, logic,
conditions, and program structure.
Features:
• Requires programming knowledge.
• Usually performed by developers.
• Focuses on code coverage and logic validation.

Key Difference:
Black Box Testing focuses on functionality, whereas White Box Testing focuses on internal code
structure and logic.
